WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.359
Sarah Mardini is relieved to be back with
her college classmates in Berlin.

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:09.820
The Syrian refugee and activist was released
on bail after spending more than three months

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.240
in a Greek prison.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.270
Sarah was returning from Lesbos in August
when police arrested her.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:21.830
She had spent the summer volunteering on the
Greek island with a pro-refugee organization.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:26.750
Because of her work helping migrants, authorities
charged her with people smuggling, money laundering

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.860
and espionage.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.270
She now faces a prison sentence of up to 25
years.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:39.690
"I never regret anything I have done and I
know deep inside that I have done that because

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.999
I wanted to help people and that’s given
me motivation."

00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:47.550
Her arrest wasn’t the first time that Sarah
made headlines.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:54.460
She caught the world’s attention in 2015
- but for very different reasons...

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.760
She and her sister Yusra were making the perilous
sea crossing from Turkey to Lesbos when their

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.710
dinghy began to take on water and sink.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:07.200
Being experienced swimmers, they jumped overboard
and pulled the waterlogged boat to the shore.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.369
By doing so, they saved the lives of the 18
people on board.

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:16.560
They then began a new life in Germany.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.751
Yusra became a goodwill ambassador for the
United Nations and was on the refugee swimming

00:01:20.751 --> 00:01:24.479
team at the 2016 Rio Olympics.

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:30.409
Sarah won a scholarship to study social sciences
in Berlin - but she kept on returning to Lesbos

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:32.260
to help others.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:36.509
"I believe when you fear something, you should
just go for it.

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:40.590
I fear the place that I landed in, and I wanted
to be on this ground again."

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:45.159
Following Sarah's arrest, her college and
classmates campaigned for her release -- organizing

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:49.869
protests in Berlin, Boston, Athens and other
cities.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:53.799
Her defense team is calling for greater protection
for humanitarian workers.

00:01:53.799 --> 00:02:01.950
"The European Union, this is an ongoing conversation
right now, has to recognize the criterion

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:15.420
of financial benefit as an obligatory requirement
in order to establish the crime of migrant

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.620
smuggling.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.280
You cannot have smugglers without benefits
and this is obvious.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:27.560
You have to exclude from sanctions people
and organizations that provide humanitarian

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.569
aid."

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:33.709
Until such safeguards are in place, Greek
authorities can claim that Sarah was helping

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:35.970
migrants enter the country illegally.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.739
She is currently waiting for her case to go
to trial.

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:45.200
She hopes it won't go that far, but is determined
to prove her innocence - and that saving lives

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.650
is not a crime.

